/*
 * The firmware release record.
 *
 * This was the third section of /record/reference/ until the reference page had
 * grown into three unrelated registers behind one local nav. Fourteen links
 * from the timeline, the explainers, the funds page and the migration record
 * pointed at its anchor rather than at the page, which is what a destination
 * looks like. /record/reference/ keeps a pointer at the old #firmware anchor.
 */
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import { modelRange, VENDOR_RANGE_AS_OF, vendorDivergences } from '../../lib/firmware';
import { BITFIG } from '../../lib/entropy-models';

/* --- Firmware releases --------------------------------------------------- */

// Checked against the firmware release histories, release commits and signed
// signed-release record. Search-space figures remain attributed estimates.
// Version boundaries interpolate from src/lib/firmware.ts, the module that
// owns the per-model ranges, so every table on this page states one set.
const mk2 = modelRange('Mk2');
const mk3 = modelRange('Mk3');
const mk4 = modelRange('Mk4');
const mk5 = modelRange('Mk5');
const q = modelRange('Q');

// Where the vendor publishes a narrower range than this site classifies, the
// same module carries the divergence, so the model table and the
// release-boundary table state one set of boundaries.
const divergence = vendorDivergences().find((d) => d.models.includes('Mk3')) ?? null;
const vendorRangeCell = divergence
  ? `<small><a href="#vendor-lower-bound">Coinkite states <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code> to <code>${divergence.affectedTo}</code></a></small>`
  : '';
const vendorStartMark = divergence
  ? `<small><a href="#vendor-lower-bound">Coinkite starts at <code>${divergence.vendorAffectedFrom}</code></a></small>`
  : '';
/*
 * The divergence used to be spelled out as a full sentence inside three table
 * cells as well as in the callout below, which is four statements of one fact
 * and made the widest cells harder to read than the disagreement warranted.
 * The compact cell markers above carry it now: they state both boundaries and
 * link to the callout, which is the canonical treatment and the only place
 * that has room for both parties' reasons.
 */
// The one-sentence summary of the chain-analysis corroboration travels with the
// range in src/lib/firmware.ts, so the tables above and this callout state it
// the same way. What belongs here rather than in the module is the verbatim
// wording. The three limits that bound it are a rung of
// their own below, so the callout states the corroboration once.
const corroborationBlock = divergence?.corroboration
  ? `
<br /><br />
One piece of outside evidence bears on the same boundary from a different direction. ${divergence.corroboration} Galaxy's wording is "The vulnerable Coldcard firmware shipped on March 17, 2021 around block 674,951. Not one of the coins we have identified in Waves 1-3 taken was created before that block." <a href="#galaxy-boundary-limits">Three limits belong with that.</a>`
  : '';
// Coinkite published its reason for the narrower boundary on 4 August 2026, in
// the disclosure history captured here on 5 August. Until then this callout
// could only record an unexplained difference. Now that both sides have stated
// a position, the callout reports both and names what they actually differ
// over, which is distribution rather than the contents of the build.
const vendorReasonBlock = divergence?.reason
  ? `
<br /><br />
<strong>Coinkite has since stated its reason.</strong> ${divergence.reason} That narrows the disagreement considerably. Both parties place the affected generation path in the 17 March 2021 build; what they disagree about is whether that build was distributed. This archive holds no evidence either way on distribution, having already recorded that the signed-release record shows 4.0.0 was built and signed but not how many devices ran it. Coinkite's account rests on records only it holds, its own signing manifest among them, and Block's wording that the path "first appeared in released firmware v4.0.0" is not accompanied by distribution evidence either. Both are reported here; neither is adjudicated.`
  : '';
const body = `<div class="table-wrap">
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="data-table sortable">
<caption>Firmware generation status by model and release range</caption>
<thead><tr>
  <th scope="col" class="nw">Model</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Firmware at generation</th>
  <th scope="col" class="nw">Status</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Candidate-space note</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-safe">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk1</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>&le; v3.0.6</code><small>final Mk1 build, 19 Dec 2019</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--ok">Not affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-safe">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">Predates the libngu migration.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-safe">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2 / Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>&le; ${mk3.lastSafe}</code><small>up to 14 Jan 2021</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--ok">Not affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-safe">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">Seed generation used <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code>, which reaches the STM32 TRNG directly. The next published release, v4.0.0, is the first one containing the affected path.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk2.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk2.affectedTo}</code><small>17 Mar 2021 to 26 Jun 2023</small>${vendorRangeCell}</td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="40">Block: <code>2^0</code> fixed state;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk23LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-gone">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">Generation moved to <code>ngu.random</code>, and this hardware has no secure-element reseed at all. Mk2 never received a v5.x release. Coinkite's advisory names this model directly, stating that the issue "is present on Mk2 and Mk3 firmware versions 4.0.1 through 4.1.9 inclusive"; that wording dates from its revision of 1 August 2026 at 18:35 UTC, before which the advisory named only the Mk3. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk3.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk3.affectedTo}</code><small>17 Mar 2021 to 26 Jun 2023</small>${vendorRangeCell}</td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="40">Block: <code>2^0</code> fixed state;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk23LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-gone">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite described the entropy as approximately 40 bits</a>; LLFOURN published a separate <code>~${BITFIG.llfournMk3}</code> model. Firmware v4.1.9 remained the last published Mk3 release until v4.2.0 was published on 31 July 2026. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">The model register states each assumption.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk4</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk4.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk4.affectedTo}</code><small>14 Mar 2022 to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-gone">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">On the normal successful boot path, <code>rng_seeding()</code> derives a value from secure-element output but carries at most 32 bits into libngu. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Coinkite, LLFOURN and otaliptus publish different broader models.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk5</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk5.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${mk5.affectedTo}</code><small>5 Mar to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-gone">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">The first repository release explicitly supporting Mk5 is v5.5.0. Both published pre-hotfix Mk5 releases are affected. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-gone">
  <td class="nw"><strong>Q</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${q.affectedFrom}</code> to <code>${q.affectedTo}</code><small>8 Feb 2024 to 1 Jul 2026</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--bad">Affected</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="32"><code>&le;${BITFIG.mk4Reseed}</code> conditional scenario;<br><code>&lt;${BITFIG.mk4LooseCeiling}</code> loose ceiling</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail row-gone">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">All Q builds recorded in the published release history before v1.5.0Q are affected, including the v1.0.0Q production release of 10 Mar 2024. <a href="/how-it-broke/entropy/">Other models use different assumptions.</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk2 / Mk3</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk3.fix}</code><small>official Mk3 X update: 31 Jul 2026, 13:43 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">The release history describes a hotfix that corrects the entropy bug and allows new seed generation. Coinkite's advisory lists the fixed release as "Mk2/Mk3: version 4.2.0 or later" and describes "Fixed Mk2/Mk3 firmware version 4.2.0" as released. Before the advisory's revision of 1 August 2026 at 18:35 UTC it named only the Mk3, and the vendor downloads-page listing of 4.2.0 for "COLDCARD Mk3 and Mk2" was the only vendor evidence covering the Mk2. The source contains the hardware-RNG fix; this archive has not reproduced the vendor build, installed it on either model or independently inspected the signed binary.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Mk4 / Mk5</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${mk4.fix}</code><small>signed release: 31 Jul 2026, 05:18 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">The signed-release record precedes source release commit <code>3238f6f</code> at 05:19 UTC. That commit contains the source-level fix. The advisory separately names Edge <code>v6.6.0X</code>; neither published binary has been independently checked here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>Q</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>${q.fix}</code><small>signed release: 31 Jul 2026, 05:17 UTC</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Published fix</span></td><td class="nw" data-sort="256">nominal 128 or 256 bits in the reviewed source</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail">The signed-release record precedes source release commit <code>3238f6f</code> at 05:19 UTC. That commit contains the source-level fix. The advisory separately names Edge <code>v6.6.0QX</code>; neither published binary has been independently checked here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>OPENDIME</strong></td><td class="nw">V3.0 r.A<small>firmware 2.2.0</small></td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">One-device test reported</span></td><td class="nw">user entropy incorporation reported for one unit</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail"><a href="/record/sources/zherbert-opendime-entropy-test/">Foundation Devices co-founder and CEO Zach Herbert reports reconstructing the final private key from the exact user-provided test vector, USB serial and revealed nonce.</a> Foundation sells competing hardware-wallet products. The test covers one unit, does not assess internal-nonce quality and was not independently reproduced here.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>TAPSIGNER</strong></td><td class="nw">All</td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Vendor-reported</span></td><td class="nw">not independently assessed here</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite reports that TAPSIGNER is not affected because it uses a different codebase.</a> <a href="/record/sources/robhamilton-opendime-tapsigner-scope/">Rob Hamilton notes that its closed source prevents first-hand verification</a> and reports only preliminary evidence that it does not use the affected libngu path.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td class="nw"><strong>SATSCARD</strong></td><td class="nw">All</td>
  <td><span class="status status--info">Vendor-reported</span></td><td class="nw">not independently assessed here</td>
</tr>
<tr class="row-detail">
  <td colspan="4" class="detail"><a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite reports that SATSCARD is not affected because it uses a different codebase.</a> This archive has not independently reviewed that codebase.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>

<p class="cap">For the named 31 July hotfixes, the source-level change is verified. The signed release files are vendor-published, but this archive has not reproduced the builds or independently inspected the binaries.</p>

<h2>Release boundary record</h2>

<p>Purchase date does not determine exposure; the published build in use at generation does. The selected releases below identify the relevant boundaries from the per-model history files.</p>

